/*
time complexity - O(nlogm) - m is max value of array - m is getting divided by 2 for binary search and for each search entire array is traversed
space complexity - O(1) - no extra space
INTUITION
You are trying to find the slowest eating speed K such that Koko still finishes all bananas within h hours.

If K is too slow, total hours exceed h → speed must increase.

If K is fast enough, total hours ≤ h → try a smaller speed to minimize K.

This creates a monotonic behavior:

As speed increases, required hours always decrease.
Which is exactly why binary search works on the "answer" space.

You binary-search the speed from 1 to max(piles), check how many hours it would take, and adjust.

*/



class Solution {
    public int minEatingSpeed(int[] piles, int h) {
        int max = Integer.MIN_VALUE;
        for(int i:piles)max= Math.max(max,i);
        int ans = Integer.MAX_VALUE;
        int l = 1, r= max;
        while(l<=r){
            int mid = (l+r)/2;
            int hrs = 0;
            for(int i=0;i<piles.length;i++){
                hrs+=Math.ceil((double)piles[i]/mid);
            }
            if(hrs<=h){
                ans = Math.min(ans,mid);
                r=mid-1;
            }
            else{
                l = mid+1;
            }
        }
        return ans;
    }
}
